Z||000025||KRMFiero||12-08-2000||07:20 PM||||Well I like it, I want it... when you get this kit ready let me know, physcokyle@aol.com OR KRMFiero@aol.com<P>btw that engine has alot of power, I have the 84 Auto... If I give it full throttle from a dead start what will happin?  Anything gonna snap or anything?, anything break or get hert?, about how fast is it? could it beat a Eclipse racing?<P>Kyle<P>------------------<BR>1984 Pontiac Fiero 2M4 SE<BR>Front - AudioBahn 4 x 10<BR>Headrest - AudioBahn 3 1/2<P> <IMG SRC="http://members.aol.com/~Physcokyle/fiero.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000026||bubbajoexx||12-08-2000||07:23 PM||||what <p>[This message has been edited by bubbajoexx (edited 12-08-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000027||bubbajoexx||12-08-2000||07:23 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Sndfr:<BR><B>OK, it's 251am on a Friday (still Thursday night to me).  For absolutly no reason i took interest in these posts by bubbajoexx.<P>So I did some research...<P><I>Outside of fabricating an engine mount, dog bone brackets and brackets for relocating the alternator, [b]the only modifications needed are trimming the lower part of the passenger side hood hinge bracket for front valve cover clearance</B>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif">. Then when using a Fiero transaxle, the bell housing must be clearanced for the Cadillac starter that mounts a little higher than the Fiero starter. No adapter plate is needed since the Cadillac engine uses the GM X-body bell housing pattern the same as the Fiero. For Auto trans the stock Cadillac flexplate is used, for manual trans, a custom flywheel must be fabricated but uses a Fiero application clutch.</I><P><BR><I>"Q. I am baffled,  these motors are very light, offer lots of torque, and they can be had very cheap! <BR>Why haven't more people taken an interest in them?  Could it be because its descended from the notoriously short lived 4.1?"<P>A. "I believe you are on the right track. I also think it may have a fair amount to do with not being an engine that has been around for a long time, people are not familiar with it and that there is not any performance parts at the local hot rod shop or catalog for it."</I><P>You can read more about it at <A HREF="http://members.tripod.com/~grannys/index.html" TARGET=_blank>Granny's Speed Shop</A>.  Go to 10-1-00 updates.<P>[/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>1 my setup uses no dog bone .2 I use a mini starter permanent magnet type  3 I chose to use the caddy engine because I had 2 front drive caddys in the past few years 4 Im no kid I have done swaps for almost 27 years no kits no web help on my own and never had one fail my first fiero swap was a 3.8 grand national turbo in 86 in a 84 fiero major work ie( changing the bock and crank reconfiguring the fuel system and turbo routing exhaust tubing nothing but on headake after another but I finished it and till today it is still running and on the road). so trying to compare some one elses info with mine in not fair or forgivable. you do not know me or know my work I have done things with cars people said are imposible yet they were completed. so not to be unfair you are entited to your op and so am I So look before you leap . <p>[This message has been edited by bubbajoexx (edited 12-08-2000).]||||reg||13
Z||000028||bubbajoexx||12-08-2000||07:39 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by KRMFiero:<BR><B>Well I like it, I want it... when you get this kit ready let me know, physcokyle@aol.com OR KRMFiero@aol.com<P>btw that engine has alot of power, I have the 84 Auto... If I give it full throttle from a dead start what will happin?  Anything gonna snap or anything?, anything break or get hert?, about how fast is it? could it beat a Eclipse racing?<P>Kyle<P><BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Ihave beaten the sh*t out of this car to try to break something  and some thing did so I talked to my bud and he told me to use a torque stap on the inside mount(mount riped) thats a gm mount for you. I have to be honest with you  my Z24 wagon beat the ? car but that was a custom built car ie By me. since the torque stap was installed no more breakage <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Z24_sm.jpg"> <p>[This message has been edited by bubbajoexx (edited 12-09-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000029||Black88GT||12-08-2000||07:56 PM||||From those super high quality pics it appears<BR>there is about an inch between the valve cover and the trunk wall.  Looks a little sloppy but maybe thats because of the pics, not much you can tell other then its a caddy motor in a Fiero.  <P>I haven't really been keeping up, but what kind of Hp does the motor put out and what year cadillacs have it? Is it aluminum?<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/cartmanico.gif">           <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kennyico.gif">         <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/stanico.gif">       <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/kyleico.gif">           <IMG SRC="http://cgi.tripod.com/smilecwm/cgi-bin/s/sp/chefico.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000030||bubbajoexx||12-08-2000||08:05 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Black88GT:<BR><B>From those super high quality pics it appears<BR>there is about an inch between the valve cover and the trunk wall.  Looks a little sloppy but maybe thats because of the pics, not much you can tell other then its a caddy motor in a Fiero.  <P>I haven't really been keeping up, but what kind of Hp does the motor put out and what year cadillacs have it? Is it aluminum?<P><BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>what high HQ FOTOS  it was a cheep thowaway camera the good fotos will be posted as I put together my next Fiero #3  The 4.1 4.5 4.9 are avalable in 86 to 93 front drive caddys all Ican give you are canadian figs 175 ,220 ,245 hp respective.<p>[This message has been edited by bubbajoexx (edited 12-08-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000031||Songman||12-09-2000||10:28 AM||||I think it is great! The only thing I will say is that I hope you have better luck with the 4.1 than most people....<P>An aluminum block engine can be a pain in the ass and those were prone to trouble... I just sold a mint 85 Eldorardo Biarritz convertible a few months ago because of the 4.1 dying on me... Lost my @ss on that car too!||||reg||1
Z||000032||bubbajoexx||12-09-2000||11:21 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Sndfr:<BR><B>OK, I'm done.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"><P><BR>[This message has been edited by Sndfr (edited 12-08-2000).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>THANK YOU Joe||||reg||6
Z||000033||bubbajoexx||12-09-2000||11:29 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Songman:<BR><B>I think it is great! The only thing I will say is that I hope you have better luck with the 4.1 than most people....<P>An aluminum block engine can be a pain in the ass and those were prone to trouble... I just sold a mint 85 Eldorardo Biarritz convertible a few months ago because of the 4.1 dying on me... Lost my @ss on that car too!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Yes there have been some prodlems with the 84 and 85  4.1 engines head gasket failure and crank failure do to oil over heating that is why the 86 and up front drives use the new grafite head gaskets and an oil cooler so far the engine has not failed even with the beating I have given it ,It's either go or blow I dont like failures and that is the only way to test if.<p>[This message has been edited by bubbajoexx (edited 12-09-2000).]||||reg||12
Z||000034||1FieroGTRacingInc||12-09-2000||11:37 AM||||Bub...   After you get one of these caddy motor's in there ,   Is still room for a Turbo??                 Thanks, Steve||||reg||1
Z||000035||Songman||12-09-2000||01:04 PM||||Sounds cool! I wish you luck! Keep us updated!||||reg||14
Z||000036||bubbajoexx||12-09-2000||01:21 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 1FieroGTRacingInc:<BR><B>Bub...   After you get one of these caddy motor's in there ,   Is still room for a Turbo??                 Thanks, Steve</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>it is possible but you will need good heat shielding around the turbo and exhaust their is a good space over the transaxle and you can tap into the cross over for the turbo and connect to the oil cooler out put to supply the oil and coolant line for cooling. but if you are going to run a turbo oring the cylinderheads and use a good graphite head gasket set.||||reg||6
Z||000037||lowCG||12-11-2000||12:12 PM||||Nice job!   I had a chance to do that swap,but had no time to research it,glad to see that my suspicions were right.I like the way it looks as if it nearly could've come from the factory that way.What are you doing for an exhaust?||||reg||1
Z||000038||bubbajoexx||12-11-2000||06:19 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by lowCG:<BR><B>Nice job!   I had a chance to do that swap,but had no time to research it,glad to see that my suspicions were right.I like the way it looks as if it nearly could've come from the factory that way.What are you doing for an exhaust?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>stock exhaust with a new cutout in the crossover to connect it to the caddy engine and a second tail pipe exiting before the muffler to give it a crisp sound ie trans am.Joe<p>[This message has been edited by bubbajoexx (edited 12-11-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000039||RedOktober||12-11-2000||08:58 PM||||SWEEET   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  Keep me posted as to when the kit details will become available.  <P>I've already had a 350 V8 with a 4 speed in a fiero, and was too scared to punch it cuz of the transaxle, and didn't feel comfortable with the added wieght. <P>How much of a difference in weight is this compared to the standard set up?<P>if going auto.. Will you use the th440?? or the stock 125C? <P>What HP Parts are available for this motor? does the TBI unit interchangable with the 350 truck units? <P>What about cooling? I've had nothing but problems even with a custom 4 pass rad and dual fans. eventually had to change the coolant tubes to fix the problem.<P>Also, what about the air box and air filter? If i remember correctly, the Caddy airbox is huge.<P>Sorry for all of the questions.. but I need enlightenment before I am willing to part with my Money   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>Thanks.<P><BR>------------------<BR>86 SE V6 Auto Turbo Going 3.4 OHV "Red Oktober"<BR>86 2M4 5sp (Beater) "Blackie"<BR>87 GT "Blue Flame"<p>[This message has been edited by RedOktober (edited 12-11-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000040||Philphine||12-11-2000||10:56 PM||||is there going to be a kit? people keep saying it but i haven't seen it offered yet.||||reg||1
Z||000041||WKDFIRO||12-12-2000||06:34 PM||||With all due respect to all those with V8 kits or kits in the works, I really miss the Zumalt V8 conversion. Maybe its because I was there when we were doing R & D for Zumalts 88 conversion kit but it was just the sweetest V8 I have seen, not some carbed up hoses everywhere V8 but the Tuned Port Injection from a 91 Vette. Its somewhere in Michigan and the owner got it for a steal. <P>Just my 2 cents, but I'll take a 3800 SC V6 before I would even consider a V8 unless Zumalt rises from the great beyond.||||reg||9
Z||000042||bubbajoexx||12-12-2000||06:53 PM||||RedOktober  As to your questions 1 the caddy engines weight about the same as a stock 2.8 V6 2 the cooling system dose not need modifing  the rad works just fine (2.5 rad) double core.Trans for autos is your choice 440 or 125 the axles in the fiero fit both.but with the 440 you must use 89 pontiac 6000 trans mounts.You can use the air breather from the 4.1 caddy it fits all of the the caddy motors. email me your addres and i will send you a package on the swap. <p>[This message has been edited by bubbajoexx (edited 12-12-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000043||bubbajoexx||12-15-2000||08:10 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Philphine:<BR><B>is there going to be a kit? people keep saying it but i haven't seen it offered yet.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>hopefully after the hollidays production will begin.||||reg||6
Z||000044||Philphine||12-15-2000||10:40 PM||||wkd, i do have to say that centering the engine when it's that heavy made sence to me. plus the open wheel well thing has never sounded that great either. if the opportunity were to arise though i'd probaly do it. there's just some thing about being able to say "it's got a v8 in it". if you were in on the development don't you have some connection to zumalt?<P>bubba, finaly you admit to a kit. i don't care really if it's a good deal and looks doable, but you could have said so in the first place. just kind of taints things a bit. an estimate of the kit price would be nice too.<P>"it's got a v8 in it" ahhhhhhhhhhh. see how it rolls off the tounge and just sort of make you feel good all over. <P><BR>say it with me. ||||reg||1
Z||000045||Will||12-15-2000||11:30 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by bubbajoexx:<BR><B>RedOktober  As to your questions 1 the caddy engines weight about the same as a stock 2.8 V6 2 the cooling system dose not need modifing  the rad works just fine (2.5 rad) double core.Trans for autos is your choice 440 or 125 the axles in the fiero fit both.but with the 440 you must use 89 pontiac 6000 trans mounts.You can use the air breather from the 4.1 caddy it fits all of the the caddy motors. email me your addres and i will send you a package on the swap. <P>[This message has been edited by bubbajoexx (edited 12-12-2000).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I see from your profile that you're from Canada. The 2.5 radiator may work well there, but will it work in Arizona, when it's 110 degrees in the shade?<BR>     Are you planning on doing any work with manual transmissions?<BR>     Do you use the stock Fiero engine mount bracket on the cradle or did you weld a new bracket to the cradle?<BR>     How thoroughly have you looked into engine movement? There is another topic in the forum right now about the inadequacy of various torque struts to control a V8.<BR>     Do you use the stock Cadillac engine management?||||reg||1
Z||000046||Will||12-15-2000||11:35 PM||||One more thing: The 4.9 was used through '95, concurrent with the Northstar.||||reg||1
Z||000047||WKDFIRO||12-16-2000||05:51 AM||||Actually the 88 GT with the 91 Vette engine (Iron block with aluminum heads) weighed in at 2910 lbs. With the power of the slightly massaged V8 (273 hp at the wheels)it took the Fiero down almost (not quite but enough to scare the hell out of unsuspecting souls.) into the Viper territory for power to weight ratio. ||||reg||6
Z||000048||bubbajoexx||12-16-2000||06:15 PM||||Will The rad from an ac equiped V6 fiero will do fine . the stock fiero front cradle mount is not changed .the use of second mount right side eliminates the torque stut a torque strap is used to backup the mount to prevent failure.||||reg||1
Z||000049||Will||12-16-2000||07:22 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by bubbajoexx:<BR><B>Will The rad from an ac equiped V6 fiero will do fine . the stock fiero front cradle mount is not changed .the use of second mount right side eliminates the torque stut a torque strap is used to backup the mount to prevent failure.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>You're profile says you're from Canada, a place not known for tropical climate. Have you tested it in hotter parts of the world?<BR>     Are we having a communications breakdown? To me "front cradle mount" means the forward points where the cradle attaches to the chassis. This is not what I was asking about.<BR>     What I gather from your description is that you're using the stock Fiero engine mount bracket on the cradle and have fabricated a bracket on the engine to bolt to the stock Fiero engine mount; you have added another mount aft of the stock Fiero engine mount, and used a torque strap to keep that from separating. What kind of mount did you use? Standard GM piece?||||reg||1
Z||000050||bubbajoexx||12-17-2000||08:40 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Will:<BR><B> You're profile says you're from Canada, a place not known for tropical climate. Have you tested it in hotter parts of the world?<BR>     Are we having a communications breakdown? To me "front cradle mount" means the forward points where the cradle attaches to the chassis. This is not what I was asking about.<BR>     What I gather from your description is that you're using the stock Fiero engine mount bracket on the cradle and have fabricated a bracket on the engine to bolt to the stock Fiero engine mount; you have added another mount aft of the stock Fiero engine mount, and used a torque strap to keep that from separating. What kind of mount did you use? Standard GM piece?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>yes i use the stock mount location in the front but i do not use the stock rubber mount I  use another mount the 2 mounts use are the same on each side . I am in the process of building my third fiero its also stick. As far as high temp is concerned I can tell you have never been to Toronto It runs in the 90's here in the summer so it ant as cold as you think. Had the motor Idle for 1 hour in 90 degree weather  out side with a stock 4 rad  and the temp never went over 90 celeus.so i cannot see you faving a problem with the larger V6 ac rad.||||reg||1
Z||000051||gduncan||12-27-2000||12:35 AM||||Just for clarification, the info quoted below<BR>that was linked from Granny's Speed Shop, <BR>came from my webpage. I have been compiling <BR>this and alot more for over two years of R&D <BR>based on my racing of a 4.9L Cadillac in a Fiero.<BR>My racing partner and I had our car at the `99 Western Regional Fiero Festival in Las Vegas, NV in April `99. I do need to update my webpage but it is based on first hand work and development of the use of a 4.9 in a Fiero. When we started this project most everyone told us we were nuts for using this engine, let alone for racing. After some mount failures we came on a set up that has worked flawlessly for 1.5 years now. We have not had one mechanical problem with the Cad 4.9 engine for the two years it has been a dedicated race car. Being driven by two drivers, 2to3 autocross events a month, plus three different trips to run on open road courses for a total of about 6 hours of flat out running on the road course. The last time was at Pheonix International Raceway's road course, running heads up with a C4 corvette &<BR>a RX7TT down the front straight at over 130 mph and gaining 2 car lenghts per lap on them<BR>through the infield for a best lap time of 1 min 14.9 secs. With the kinds of negative reactions we got when we started this, we did<BR>not expect to see the interest that is now all across the country due to what my racing partner Tom Hill and I have done with our car. We are developing a Kit for 4.9 conversions, the mount kit will be first and if all goes well I hope to have it at Ed Parks Fiero Factory Swapmeet in `01.<P>Greg Duncan<BR>Other half of Team Python <A HREF="http://www.azstarnet.com/~gduncan" TARGET=_blank>http://www.azstarnet.com/~gduncan</A> <P><BR>&gt;Originally posted by Sndfr:<BR>&gt;OK, it's 251am on a Friday (still Thursday &gt;night to me). For absolutly no reason i took<BR>&gt;interest in these posts by bubbajoexx.<BR>&gt;<BR>&gt;So I did some research...<BR>&gt;<BR>&gt;Outside of fabricating an engine mount, dog <BR>&gt;bone brackets and brackets for relocating <BR>&gt;the alternator, <B>the only modifications <BR>&gt;needed are trimming the lower part of the <BR>&gt;passenger side hood hinge bracket for front <BR>&gt;valve cover clearance. Then when using a <BR>&gt;Fiero transaxle, the bell housing must be <BR>&gt;clearanced for the Cadillac starter that <BR>&gt;mounts a little higher than the Fiero <BR>&gt;starter. No adapter plate is needed since <BR>&gt;the Cadillac engine uses the GM X-body bell <BR>&gt;housing pattern the same as the Fiero. For <BR>&gt;Auto trans the stock Cadillac flexplate is <BR>&gt;used, for manual trans, a custom flywheel <BR>&gt;must be fabricated but uses a Fiero <BR>&gt;application clutch.<BR>&gt;<BR>&gt;<BR>&gt;"Q. I am baffled, these motors are very <BR>&gt;light, offer lots of torque, and they can be<BR>&gt;had very cheap!<BR>&gt;Why haven't more people taken an interest in<BR>&gt; them? Could it be because its descended <BR>&gt;from the notoriously short lived 4.1?"<BR>&gt;<BR>&gt;A. "I believe you are on the right track. I <BR>&gt;also think it may have a fair amount to do <BR>&gt;with not being an engine that has been <BR>&gt;around for a long time, people are not <BR>&gt;familiar with it and that there is not any <BR>&gt;performance parts at the local hot rod shop <BR>&gt;or catalog for it."<BR>&gt;<BR>&gt;<BR>&gt;You can read more about it at Granny's Speed<BR>&gt;Shop. Go to 10-1-00 updates.<BR>&gt;<BR>&gt;</B><P>||||reg||1
Z||000052||horseman||12-27-2000||02:17 AM||||Wow Greg,<BR>that sounds impressive! I can't wait to read more info on your swap. What kind of numbers are you putting on a dyno? I like the fact that your swap has been "track tested". <BR>Please e-mail me with the details when they are available.<BR>Sincerely,<BR>Charlie||||reg||1
Z||000053||bubbajoexx||12-28-2000||04:26 PM||||[QUOTE]Originally posted by gduncan:<BR>[B]Just for clarification, the info quoted below<BR>that was linked from Granny's Speed Shop, <BR>came from my webpage. I have been compiling <BR>this and alot more for over two years of R <BR>based on my racing of a 4.9L Cadillac in a Fiero.<BR>My racing partner and I had our car at the `99 Western Regional Fiero Festival in Las Vegas, NV in April `99. I do need to update my webpage but it is based on first hand work and development of the use of a 4.9 in a Fiero. When we started this project most everyone told us we were nuts for using this engine, let alone for racing. After some mount failures we came on a set up that has worked flawlessly for 1.5 years now. We have not had one mechanical problem with the Cad 4.9 engine for the two years it has been a dedicated race car. Being driven by two drivers, 2to3 autocross events a month, plus three different trips to run on open road courses for a total of about 6 hours of flat out running on the road course. The last time was at Pheonix International Raceway's road course, running heads up with a C4 corvette &<BR>a RX7TT down the front straight at over 130 mph and gaining 2 car lenghts per lap on them<BR>through the infield for a best lap time of 1 min 14.9 secs. With the kinds of negative reactions we got when we started this, we did<BR>not expect to see the interest that is now all across the country due to what my racing partner Tom Hill and I have done with our car. We are developing a Kit for 4.9 conversions, the mount kit will be first and if all goes well I hope to have it at Ed Parks Fiero Factory Swapmeet in `01.<P>Greg Duncan<BR>good luck with your kit <BR> you have been racing with this engine for 2 years Ihave been installing this engine in cavaleirs since 86 when i got one for free and had a 4 banger cav so i put it in lots of work and have done it since so i am not inexperianced with this engine family experence counts so GOOD LUCK JOE not known as BUBBA for nothing<BR><p>[This message has been edited by bubbajoexx (edited 12-28-2000).]||||reg||1
